: build -- install OpenCoherent onto the hard disk of a Commodore 900.
:
: Run from the installation floppy, which is the running root filesystem.
: The shape is the original: warn, scan the target for bad blocks, make a
: filesystem, mount it, copy the medium onto it, and hand the machine back
: to the user to reboot off the hard disk.
:
: WHICH DEVICES.  /dev/hd0 is the boot partition the ROM reads, /dev/hd4 the
: root filesystem, /dev/hd1 is /usr, /dev/hd2 is /usr/man, /dev/hd3 is /tmp
: and swap.  That is the partition table this floppy handed the kernel at
: boot out of its own kboot.cfg, so these are the same partitions the
: installed system will come up on.
:
: NOTE for anyone editing this file: this shell has no comment character.
: Every line above begins with the null command, so none of them may contain
: a shell metacharacter -- a parenthesis in a comment is a syntax error.
echo 'This will destroy everything on the hard disk.'
echo 'If you have files to save, interrupt now with the DEL key.'
echo 'To build the hard disk system, press RETURN.'
read j

: THE BAD-BLOCK SCAN IS NOT RUN HERE, and it should be.  The original step
: pipes a prototype through /etc/mkproto on the RAW root device, which reads
: every block and puts a %b list of the ones that fail into the prototype it
: writes out.  On this port it cannot: wd RAW transfers
: require the user buffer to be 512-byte aligned, mkproto reads into an
: ordinary static array, and the driver rejects the transfer -- so mkproto
: fails on the first block and writes a truncated prototype.  Until raw
: reads bounce an unaligned buffer, the filesystems below are made from the
: generated prototypes as they stand, with no bad blocks named.  badscan is
: on this medium and has the same limitation.
echo 'Writing the root filesystem...'
/etc/mkfs /dev/hd4 /kit/root.proto
/etc/mount /dev/hd4 /mnt

: The medium copies onto the new root wholesale, device nodes included --
: cpdir recreates those with mknod.  /mnt is skipped because it is the mount
: point this copy is going through, /tmp because it holds scratch for this
: run, and /kit because the installer does not belong on the system it
: installs.
echo 'Copying the system to the hard disk...'
/bin/cpdir -d -smnt -stmp -skit / /mnt

: On the hard disk the kernel is /coherent and no loader sits beside it: the
: loader lives on the boot partition, written below.  So the copy that the
: medium made of its own /coherent -- kboot -- is replaced by the kernel.
:
: AND IT IS /hdcoherent, not the kernel this floppy is running.  They are one
: kernel built once and staged twice: this one roots on the floppy, that one
: on the hard disk.  Installing the floppy variant leaves a machine that
: panics with  fsminit: no rootdev  as soon as the drive is empty.
/bin/rm -f /mnt/coherent.sys /mnt/hdcoherent /mnt/kboot.cfg
/bin/cp /hdcoherent /mnt/coherent

: LATER VOLUMES ARE NOT READ HERE, and cannot be.  This medium is the mounted
: root filesystem while the installer runs, so swapping it for volume 2 would
: leave the kernel writing cached blocks of one disc onto another.  The
: answer in the original -- and there is only one drive to answer with -- is to
: finish the root filesystem, reset, and read the rest once the hard disk is
: root and the drive is free.  /kit/cpsys is that second stage: it is
: installed as /.profile on the new system, so the first login runs it, and it
: replaces itself when the last volume is in.
: The .profile belonging to root is parked as /etc/pro.def and cpsys takes
: its place, and the last act of cpsys is to move it back.  The volume list
: goes with it, because the second stage runs on a machine with no /kit -- the
: installer does not belong on the system it installs.
/bin/mv /mnt/.profile /mnt/etc/pro.def
/bin/cp /kit/cpsys /mnt/.profile
/bin/cp /kit/volumes /mnt/etc/volumes
/bin/mkdir /mnt/f0

: The other filesystems.  Their mount points are made whatever happens: the
: /etc/rc of the installed system mounts /usr, and a mount point that does not
: exist is a mount that does not happen.
echo 'Writing the remaining filesystems...'
/etc/mkfs /dev/hd1 /kit/usr.proto
/etc/mkfs /dev/hd2 /kit/man.proto
/etc/mkfs /dev/hd3 /kit/tmp.proto
/bin/mkdir /mnt/usr
/bin/mkdir /mnt/usr/man
/bin/sync
/etc/umount /dev/hd4

echo 'Writing the boot partition...'
/etc/mkfs /dev/hd0 /kit/boot.proto
/etc/mount /dev/hd0 /mnt
/bin/cp /coherent /mnt/coherent
/bin/cp /kit/kboot.cfg /mnt/kboot.cfg
/bin/sync
/etc/umount /dev/hd0

: The prototypes name the sizes, so nothing on this medium restates the
: layout: /kit/NAME.proto is generated from the target media descriptor, one
: per filesystem, by the same packer that generated /kit/kboot.cfg.
echo 'The hard disk system is installed.'
echo 'Remove the floppy and reset the machine.  With no floppy in the drive'
echo 'the ROM boots the hard disk, and the system you just installed comes up.'
